    "personId": "charles_godfrey",
    "transcript": "Charles Godfrey Brenay, also recorded as Gottfried and under the surname Brunne, was born in Germany around 1873. His exact birthplace is still unknown. He entered the world just after German unification, as railways, factories and new national institutions were transforming central Europe.\n\nDuring his generation, millions of Europeans crossed the Atlantic. Steamships shortened the voyage, while expanding rail networks carried migrants inland after arrival. Charles joined that movement, but his departure point, ship and precise route have not survived. An 1874 photograph of German emigrants preserves the human scale of that migration, although it does not show Charles or establish his port.\n\nBy the 1890s, Charles was living in Ontario with Ida Mae Gooley. Their son Charles Albert was born in Arnstein in 1897. Arnstein was a small settlement in the forested Parry Sound District, where farms, timber work and scattered communities connected southward toward Georgian Bay and the Great Lakes.\n\nThe household remained in Ontario at the opening of the twentieth century. Across the border, Michigan’s lumber and manufacturing towns drew workers and families from Canada and Europe. Around 1903, the Brenays moved into the United States and settled in Alpena.\n\nAlpena stood on Lake Huron, surrounded by sawmills, docks, rail lines and streets filled with immigrant households. The city was rebuilding and diversifying as the great pine forests that had powered its early growth were cut away. Charles’s family became part of this changing Great Lakes community. Period photographs of Alpena’s brick business blocks and immense lumber yards preserve the changing city into which the family settled.\n\nAcross borders and languages, Brunne became Brenay, while Gottfried and Godfrey appeared beside Charles. The changing forms of his name reflect a life lived across German and English-speaking worlds. In Alpena, he entered the naturalization process as he established a permanent place in the United States.\n\nCharles and Ida’s children carried both sides of that borderland history: German ancestry, an Ontario household and an American future. Their son Charles Albert continued the family’s movement west, eventually taking the Brenay line from Michigan to Arizona.\n\nWhen Charles Godfrey died in 1942, the Atlantic crossing of his youth had become the beginning of a much longer family journey. Germany, Ontario and Michigan remained joined in the name and history his descendants carried forward.",
