    "personId": "john_peter",
    "transcript": "John Peter Gooley was born on May 2, 1842, in Quebec. His father was remembered as Peter or Pierre Gooley and his mother as Louise, but the family’s exact parish has not yet been identified. His story therefore begins within the wider St. Lawrence world rather than at a particular town.\n\nQuebec was then part of the newly created Province of Canada, twenty-five years before Confederation. Farms, parish communities and market towns were tied together by rivers and canals, while the first railways were beginning to alter travel across British North America. An 1856 photograph of a Quebec farm preserves part of that material world, though it does not identify John Peter’s family, home or parish.\n\nJohn Peter grew up as those transportation networks expanded. Steamships, canals and rail lines gradually connected Quebec more closely with Ontario and the American Great Lakes, making movement westward easier than it had been in his parents’ generation. A period Ontario railway-station photograph records the transportation transformation surrounding his westward years, without establishing his particular route.\n\nHe eventually left Quebec and lived in Ontario. He married Mary Ann Dennis, who had been born in Ontario in 1844. Together they formed a household whose Canadian roots crossed both the French-speaking and English-speaking worlds of the province.\n\nJohn Peter and Mary Ann later moved into Michigan. By 1872 they were living in Alpena, where their daughter Ida Mae was born. Alpena was a young lumber and shipping town on Lake Huron, connected to Ontario by water, commerce and family movement.\n\nOne day after Ida’s birth, fire swept through Alpena, destroying homes, businesses and mills. The town rebuilt as lumber production, lake shipping and rail connections expanded. John Peter’s family lived within a community changing almost as quickly as the transportation system that had carried families around the Great Lakes. A surviving photograph of Alpena after the fire preserves the damaged town, though no Gooley home or family member is identified.\n\nDuring John Peter’s lifetime, the Province of Canada became the Dominion of Canada, the United States passed through civil war and reconstruction, and the Great Lakes borderland became increasingly industrial. His own path—from Quebec through Ontario to Michigan—followed the same broad westward current.\n\nJohn Peter lived until 1910. His daughter Ida joined the Gooley line to the German-born Brenay family, and his Canadian beginnings continued through their son Charles Albert and into the later Webb family in Arizona.\n\nBefore John Peter, the trail remains unfinished. Peter or Pierre and Louise are the strongest names carried forward, but their identities and John Peter’s Quebec baptism remain unproved. His life marks both a Canadian foundation and the present edge of the documented Gooley story.",
