    "transcript": "Mary Ann Dennis was born on August 15, 1844, in Ontario. Her exact birthplace and parents remain unresolved, so her story begins at the scale of the province rather than a particular town.\n\nOntario was then known as Canada West, part of the Province of Canada under British rule. Farms and small settlements spread along waterways and early roads, while canals, steamships and the first railways began binding the region more closely to Quebec and the Great Lakes.\n\nMary Ann grew up during that transformation. By adulthood, trains crossed southern Ontario and lake steamers carried passengers, freight and news between Canadian and American ports. The international border was real, but daily life around the lakes often extended across it. A period Ontario railway-station photograph preserves the changing transportation world of her adulthood, without identifying Mary Ann’s town, station or route.\n\nShe married John Peter Gooley, who had been born in Quebec in 1842. Their household joined two Canadian beginnings: Mary Ann’s Ontario world and John Peter’s Quebec origins. They later moved into Michigan, continuing the westward movement that shaped many Great Lakes families.\n\nBy 1872, Mary Ann and John Peter were living in Alpena, a young lumber and shipping town on Lake Huron. Their daughter Ida Mae was born there on July 11. One day later, fire swept through the town and destroyed homes, businesses and mills. A surviving photograph of Alpena after the fire records the damaged town, although no Dennis-Gooley property or person is identified.\n\nAlpena rebuilt around timber, docks, workshops and expanding rail connections. Mary Ann raised her family in a place where the landscape and economy were changing rapidly, and where Canadian-born residents lived alongside immigrants arriving from Europe and other parts of North America. Later photographs of Alpena’s rebuilt commercial blocks and lumber yards show the civic and industrial setting that grew around the family.\n\nThrough Ida Mae, Mary Ann’s Ontario birth became part of the Brenay family. Ida married the German-born Charles Godfrey Brenay, lived in both Michigan and Ontario, and gave birth to Charles Albert at Arnstein in 1897.\n\nMary Ann lived until 1919. During her lifetime, Canada West became Ontario, the Dominion of Canada was created, railways spread across the continent and the Great Lakes borderland became increasingly industrial.\n\nThe names of Mary Ann’s parents are still missing, but her place in the documented family is strong. Her life carried the Dennis line from Ontario into Michigan and, through later generations, onward to Arizona.",
